@page "/reports"
@model PMS.Web.Pages.Reports.IndexModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Reports";
}

<div class="pms-page-header">
    <div>
        <h1>Reports</h1>
        <p class="pms-page-header__subtitle">
            Sales, profit and what is sitting on the shelves. Every figure here is costed from the
            specific batch each sale came out of.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="report-cards">
    <a class="report-card" asp-page="/Reports/DailySales">
        <h2>Daily sales</h2>
        <p>
            One day's takings, profit and every invoice, with an hourly breakdown showing when the
            counter is busy.
        </p>
    </a>

    <a class="report-card" asp-page="/Reports/MonthlySales">
        <h2>Monthly sales &amp; profit</h2>
        <p>
            A month day by day, with gross and net profit and the trend across the month.
        </p>
    </a>

    <a class="report-card" asp-page="/Reports/TopSelling">
        <h2>Top selling products</h2>
        <p>
            What moved, net of returns &mdash; sortable by quantity, revenue or profit, because
            the three rarely name the same product.
        </p>
    </a>

    <a class="report-card" asp-page="/Reports/ByProductType">
        <h2>Sales by product type</h2>
        <p>
            How much of the margin comes from medicines and how much from everything else.
        </p>
    </a>

    <a class="report-card" asp-page="/Reports/DeadStock">
        <h2>Dead stock</h2>
        <p>
            Products that have not sold in months, or ever, and how much capital is tied up in
            them.
        </p>
    </a>

    <a class="report-card" asp-page="/Reports/SalesPerUser">
        <h2>Sales per user</h2>
        <p>
            What each person at the counter rang up, and how much discount they gave.
        </p>
    </a>

    <a class="report-card" asp-page="/Reports/StockValuation">
        <h2>Stock valuation</h2>
        <p>
            What the shelves are worth at cost right now, with expired stock counted separately.
        </p>
    </a>

    <a class="report-card" asp-page="/Reports/SupplierDues">
        <h2>Supplier dues</h2>
        <p>
            What is owed to each supplier, with what has been purchased, paid and returned.
        </p>
    </a>
</div>

<div class="report-note">
    <h2 class="h6">About these figures</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Cost comes from the batch each sale drew from</strong>, not from the product's
            current price. Two deliveries of the same medicine bought at different prices are
            costed separately, which is the whole reason batches exist.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Revenue is after discount.</strong> A bill-level discount is spread across its
            lines, so a discounted sale reports the margin actually earned on it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Cancelled sales are excluded, not counted as zero.</strong> They never
            happened, so they are absent from totals, counts and averages alike.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A return reduces the period it happened in</strong>, not the period of the
            original sale &mdash; matching when the cash actually went back out.
        </li>
        <li>
            Dates are bounded in UTC, the same as everywhere else in the system.
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>
